            <ArticleTitle>Rapidly enlarging lipoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-221272</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-221272</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Lipoma is a common subcutaneous tumour composed of fat tissue. It may occur as a sporadic solitary lesion or as multiple lesions. They often grow very slowly. They vary between 2 and 10 cm in size. There are rarely any subjective symptoms. Lipomas do not usually require treatment unless they are big enough to be symptomatic. We reported a 75-year-old man with a rapidly enlarging and ulcerated mass on his right upper thigh.</AbstractText>
